{"source":"user-event","type":"click","text":"A\n    \n      Full Pivot: PDF Fixer Only\n      Build a tool that takes a broken-bidi PDF (from LinkedIn, etc.) and outputs a corrected PDF with proper RTL direction.\n      How: User uploads PDF → we parse it with pdf-lib → detect and fix text direction → output corrected PDF\n      Form: Could be a web app, CLI tool, or Chrome extension that intercepts PDF downloads\n      \n        Pros\n          Solves an unsolved problem\n          Clear differentiation — no competition\n          High value for Hebrew job seekers\n        \n        Cons\n          PDF parsing is complex (fonts, encoding)\n          Narrower use case\n          Different tech stack needed","choice":"a","id":null,"timestamp":1774886539531}
{"source":"user-event","type":"click","text":"A\n    \n      Full Pivot: PDF Fixer Only\n      Build a tool that takes a broken-bidi PDF (from LinkedIn, etc.) and outputs a corrected PDF with proper RTL direction.\n      How: User uploads PDF → we parse it with pdf-lib → detect and fix text direction → output corrected PDF\n      Form: Could be a web app, CLI tool, or Chrome extension that intercepts PDF downloads\n      \n        Pros\n          Solves an unsolved problem\n          Clear differentiation — no competition\n          High value for Hebrew job seekers\n        \n        Cons\n          PDF parsing is complex (fonts, encoding)\n          Narrower use case\n          Different tech stack needed","choice":"a","id":null,"timestamp":1774886540704}
{"source":"user-event","type":"click","text":"B\n    \n      Hybrid: Smart Web Fix + PDF Export (Recommended)\n      Keep the smart web bidi fixer, BUT add a killer feature: \"Download as fixed PDF\". When user clicks download on LinkedIn etc., intercept and generate a clean RTL-correct PDF from the fixed DOM.\n      How: Fix DOM first (our existing plan) → when user wants PDF, use the fixed page to generate a proper PDF via html2canvas + jsPDF or browser print API\n      \n        Pros\n          Best of both worlds\n          Web fixing is still useful (RTL Toggle is dumb toggle, we're smarter)\n          PDF export is the differentiator\n          Uses DOM we already fixed — less PDF parsing complexity\n        \n        Cons\n          Larger scope\n          PDF from DOM won't be identical to LinkedIn's layout","choice":"b","id":null,"timestamp":1774886578351}
